{"name":"Organic","hex":"#747261","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"organic","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Pertaining to or derived from living organisms. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Pertaining to an organ of the body of a living organism."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An organic compound.","An organic food."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/organic"}],"entries":[{"title":"Organ (biology)","blocks":[{"text":"In a multicellular organism, an organ is a collection of tissues joined in a structural unit to serve a common function. In the hierarchy of life, an organ lies between tissue and an organ system. Tissues are formed from same type cells to act together in a function. Tissues of different types combine to form an organ which has a specific function. The intestinal wall, for example, is formed by epithelial tissue and smooth muscle tissue. Two or more organs working together in the execution of a specific body function form an organ system, also called a biological system or body system."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Internal_organs.png/330px-Internal_organs.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Organ_(biology)","wikidata":"Q712378"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Internal_organs.png/500px-Internal_organs.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Internal_organs.png/1280px-Internal_organs.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Internal_organs.png/500px-Internal_organs.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AInternal_organs.png"},{"srcset":"https: